---
import KusaHero from "../components/KusaHero.astro";
import "../styles/kusa-landing.css";
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <meta name="description" content="Kusa builds software that works automatically." />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#1d2021" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="Kusa — Automatic software" />
    <meta property="og:description" content="Kusa builds software that works automatically." />
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:image" content="/og.png" />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image" />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/brand/apple-touch-icon.png" />
    <title>Kusa — Automatic software</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>
      <KusaHero>
        <section class="site-section" id="about" hidden>
          <p class="section-lede" tabindex="-1">
            Technology should empower humans, never replace them.
          </p>
          <p class="section-body">
            Kusa is built on that belief. We tap into our partners&rsquo;
            expertise and automate the boring parts of their day: critical
            infrastructure that stays reliable and bulletproof, and apps that
            spark joy instead of tickets. The hours we win back belong to
            people, for the work only people can do.
          </p>
          <p class="section-body">
            <em>Humans stay at the wheel.</em> AI is the sharpest tool
            we&rsquo;ve ever picked up, and a tool is all it is. We still read
            every line of code; the speed AI buys us gets spent on validation,
            testing, and reliability.
          </p>
          <p class="section-body">
            <em>Joy that outlasts the demo.</em> The best tools disappear into
            the work and leave people better at it. We build software people
            enjoy using, and systems their owners actually understand.
          </p>
          <p class="section-body">The humans behind kusa:</p>
          <ul class="section-list">
            <li class="founder">
              <img class="founder-photo" src="/founders/rafael-gutierrez.svg" alt="Rafael Gutierrez" width="96" height="96" />
              <span>
                <a class="section-link" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/paelgutierrez/">Rafael Gutierrez</a>:
                generalist who&rsquo;s shipped across the stack at bleeding-edge
                startups (Akkio, Aescape), happiest building internal tools that
                make validation fast.
              </span>
            </li>
            <li class="founder">
              <img class="founder-photo" src="/founders/daniel-trinidad.svg" alt="Daniel Trinidad" width="96" height="96" />
              <span>
                <a class="section-link" href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/daniel-trinidad-a12ab386/">Daniel Trinidad</a>:
                software engineer with nearly a decade in enterprise
                integrations and microservices (BMTG, OOCL); led migrations off
                legacy platforms and coached teams toward better ways of working.
              </span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="site-section" id="values" hidden>
          <p class="section-lede" tabindex="-1">
            What we hold ourselves to:
          </p>
          <ul class="section-list">
            <li>
              <em>The agency is ours.</em> Tools execute; humans intend. We
              keep our minds sharp and never outsource the thinking.
            </li>
            <li>
              <em>Bring lightness.</em> Warmth goes a long way, especially
              under stress. We overcommunicate, because nobody reads minds as
              well as we assume.
            </li>
            <li>
              <em>Lead with kindness.</em> People first, workers second.
              Everyone has bad days; assume grace, and generosity compounds.
            </li>
            <li>
              <em>Show the cracks.</em> Openness builds trust, concealment
              builds distance. A mistake revealed is a win, not a charge.
            </li>
            <li>
              <em>Never stop asking.</em> Humble enough to know how much we
              don&rsquo;t know, confident enough to question what everyone
              knows. We teach what we learn.
            </li>
            <li>
              <em>Code is a tool, not the point.</em> We&rsquo;re not here to
              sell clever code. If a spreadsheet ran the business better,
              we&rsquo;d use the spreadsheet.
            </li>
            <li>
              <em>Big dreams, sober plans.</em> Ambition is cheap; the plan is
              the hard part. We convince ourselves of every step before we take
              it.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <section class="site-section" id="posts" hidden>
          <p class="section-lede" tabindex="-1">
            Nothing here yet. First post soon.
          </p>
        </section>

        <section class="site-section" id="contact" hidden>
          <p class="section-lede" tabindex="-1">
            Have something that should run itself?
          </p>
          <a class="section-link" href="mailto:hello@kusa.build">hello@kusa.build</a>
        </section>
      </KusaHero>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
